## Add an article

Create a `.svx` file in `src/content`. Its frontmatter supplies the title, description, slug, section and order. The content registry discovers that metadata and the article body.

```yaml
---
slug: your-first-guide
title: Your first guide
description: A short, useful description of the task.
section: Get started
order: 4
---
```

Add its slug to `src/lib/content/navigation.ts` to place it in the sidebar. The search index, Markdown output and page metadata use the content registry.

## Use interactive content

Import `CodeTabs`, `Callout` or `Diagram` in an article’s script. CodeTabs accepts a record of language names and code strings. Examples share a language preference; set `sync={false}` for an independent example.

## Change the visual system

`src/app.css` owns the color tokens, typography and common primitives. Component styles own their layout. Keep the light, dark and glass token sets aligned rather than adding theme conditions to every control.

## Replace learning content

`src/lib/content/learning.ts` defines glossary categories, terms, lab units and sequence exercises. Browser progress references stable identifiers. Change the storage version deliberately when you replace those identities.

## Connect an assistant

Without configuration, Ask OSA finds matching passages from local documentation. To connect a model, set `DOCS_AI_BASE_URL`, `DOCS_AI_KEY` and `DOCS_AI_MODEL` on the server. The base URL should expose an OpenAI-compatible `/chat/completions` endpoint.

Keys never go into browser code. The response identifies whether it came from the configured model or the local document finder. If a service request fails, the panel offers a retry and keeps the question visible.

## Build and run

```bash
npm install
npm run check
npm run build
node build
```

Use the Node adapter for the API and assistant routes. A purely static export would need those endpoints moved to another service.
